### Install and configure Tailwind CSS v4 with PostCSS

Source: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com/blob/main/src/blog/tailwindcss-v4/index.mdx

Follow this three-step workflow to install Tailwind CSS v4 and its PostCSS plugin, register the plugin in PostCSS configuration, and import Tailwind in CSS without extra configuration files.

```shell
# [!code filename:1. Install Tailwind CSS]
npm i tailwindcss @tailwindcss/postcss;
```

```js
// [!code filename:2. Add the PostCSS plugin]
export default {
  plugins: ["@tailwindcss/postcss"],
};
```

```css
/* [!code filename:3. Import Tailwind in your CSS] */
@import "tailwindcss";
```

--------------------------------

### Configure PostCSS with @tailwindcss/postcss in postcss.config.mjs

Source: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com/blob/main/src/docs/upgrade-guide.mdx

Update PostCSS configuration to use the dedicated @tailwindcss/postcss package. Remove postcss-import and autoprefixer as import handling and vendor prefixing are included automatically in v4.

```js
// [!code filename:postcss.config.mjs]
export default {
  plugins: {
    // [!code --:4]
    "postcss-import": {},
    tailwindcss: {},
    autoprefixer: {},
    // [!code ++:2]
    "@tailwindcss/postcss": {},
  },
};
```

--------------------------------

### Configuring postcss.config.js with built-in import support

Source: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com/blob/main/src/blog/tailwindcss-v4/index.mdx

Remove `postcss-import` from your PostCSS configuration when using `@tailwindcss/postcss`, as CSS import inlining is handled natively.

```javascript
//  [!code filename:postcss.config.js]
export default {
  plugins: [
    "postcss-import", // [!code --]
    "@tailwindcss/postcss",
  ],
};
```

--------------------------------

### Ignoring specific paths with @source not

Source: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com/blob/main/src/docs/detecting-classes-in-source-files.mdx

Excludes specific relative directory paths from being scanned for Tailwind utility classes. Useful for skipping legacy components or third-party code.

```css
/* [!code filename:CSS] */
@import "tailwindcss";
/* [!code highlight:2] */
@source not "../src/components/legacy";
```

--------------------------------

### Upgrading manually > Using PostCSS

Source: https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com/blob/main/src/docs/upgrade-guide.mdx

In v3, the `tailwindcss` package was a PostCSS plugin, but in v4 the PostCSS plugin lives in a dedicated `@tailwindcss/postcss` package.

Additionally, in v4 imports and vendor prefixing is now handled for you automatically, so you can remove `postcss-import` and `autoprefixer` if they are in your project:

